<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  OWNER: Aayush. Task A7.2 — Focus Guard accessibility service configuration.

  EVERY ATTRIBUTE HERE IS SET TO TAKE AS LITTLE AS POSSIBLE:

  accessibilityEventTypes="typeWindowStateChanged"
      The one event that says "a different app is in front". Not typeAllMask, which would deliver
      view clicks, text changes and scrolls — i.e. the contents of everything the user does on the
      phone — for no benefit to this feature.

  canRetrieveWindowContent="false"
      Ally never reads the screen. This is the attribute that makes that a platform guarantee
      rather than a promise in a comment: with it false, getRootInActiveWindow() returns nothing
      even if someone later adds a call to it.

  notificationTimeout="0"
      No throttling. This value is the minimum gap the framework enforces between similar events,
      so any non-zero value is added directly to how long a restricted app stays on screen before
      Ally reacts — and that delay is the whole question this feature lives or dies on. The
      anti-loop debounce is done in FocusGuardService, where it can be scoped per package, instead.

  No android:packageNames filter
      Scoping the service to the two demo packages here would be tighter still, but the restricted
      list is configurable at runtime (A7.9) and this file is baked in at build time, so the two
      would drift. The runtime check in FocusGuard.restrictedLabel() is the real filter. A
      production version should narrow this with setServiceInfo() when the list changes.
-->
<accessibility-service xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:accessibilityEventTypes="typeWindowStateChanged"
    android:accessibilityFeedbackType="feedbackGeneric"
    android:canRetrieveWindowContent="false"
    android:description="@string/ally_focus_guard_description"
    android:notificationTimeout="0" />
